// BT-3163: BT3163ComparisonGuardActor — an `ensure:` try body whose LAST
// statement is a comparison operator (`<`) on operands not statically known
// comparable (untyped params), forcing `generate_binary_op`'s runtime
// `beamtalk_primitive:is_object/1` guard (`operators.rs::guarded_op_doc`).
// That guard's `case` matched only `<'true'>`/`<'false'>` with no wildcard
// clause — the same non-exhaustive-to-the-compiler shape BT-3161 fixed for
// self-dispatch and boolean `ifTrue:`/`ifFalse:` cases — and, nested inside
// the `try`'s protected region, tripped the same OTP `beam_validator`
// `ambiguous_catch_try_state` bug. Unlike the arithmetic (`is_number`) guard,
// which the Core Erlang compiler proves exhaustive via BIF return-type
// inference, `is_object` is a plain function the compiler cannot prove
// exhaustive, so the implicit fallback — and the bug — were real here. See
// `operators.rs::guarded_op_doc`'s doc comment for the full analysis.

Actor subclass: BT3163ComparisonGuardActor
  state: count = 0

  // BT-3163 repro: ensure: try body's last statement is a comparison
  // operator forcing the is_object runtime guard.
  compareUnsafe: a with: b =>
    [
      self.count := self.count + 1
      a < b
    ] ensure: [nil]

  // NLR passthrough through the same shape: an early `^` fires before the
  // comparison (still the try body's last statement) is ever reached, and
  // must still escape the `ensure:` unaffected by the wildcard fallback
  // clause the comparison guard's `case` now carries.
  compareUnsafeNlr: flag with: a with: b =>
    [
      flag ifTrue: [^"early"]
      self.count := self.count + 1
      a < b
    ] ensure: [nil]
    "not early"

  getCount => self.count
